window.__hvBdRegister("plot-e6", {"key":"plot-e6","sections":[{"i":0,"j":37,"loc":"First Tractate 1–5","title":"The ten categories questioned: Substance and Quantity","summary":"Plotinus surveys earlier views on the number of Existents and takes up the tenfold division of the categories, asking whether it covers both the Intellectual and the Sensible realms; he notes that its authors overlooked the Supreme. He argues that Substance cannot be a single genus common to both realms, and, though he looks for something common to Matter, Form and Composite, finds that its listed properties group substances under a common name without establishing a genus or revealing their nature. Turning to Quantity, he holds that only number is Quantity in the strict sense, while magnitudes, space, time and motion are quantities only secondarily. Speech is treated as a significant action rather than a quantity, and time is said not to be Quantity in its essence.","ideas":["The ten categories ignore the Intellectual realm and use 'Being' equivocally","Substance is a grouping by common name, not a true genus","Only number is Quantity proper; magnitudes are quantities by approximation","Speech is an action with significance; time is measured, not essentially Quantity"],"terms":[{"t":"genus","d":"A class predicated of its species in respect of their essential character, as opposed to a mere category grouping things by name."},{"t":"First and Second Substances","d":"The individual substance and the species or genus predicated of it, whose common basis Plotinus questions."}]},{"i":38,"j":68,"loc":"First Tractate 6–9","title":"Does Relation have any reality?","summary":"Plotinus asks whether Relation, as in double and half, father and son, right and left, has actuality or is only our own comparison of things. He first argues that likeness, equality and position seem to add nothing to the objects, then counters that doubleness and equality hold before we notice them, so Relation is real. Correlatives like double and half arise together from a certain conjunction of objects, though some relations survive the loss of one term. He concludes that relations are Reason-Principles participating in Ideal-Forms whose causes lie in the higher sphere, yet the category of Relation, which includes negations and derivatives, cannot be one genus.","ideas":["Relation as mere comparison versus Relation as a fact existing before our judgement","Double and half emerge simultaneously from a conjunction of objects","Relations are Reason-Principles grounded in Ideal-Forms such as Doubleness","One category of Relation, but not a single genus"],"terms":[{"t":"correlatives","d":"Terms like double and half whose being consists in mutual dependence."},{"t":"Reason-Principle (logos)","d":"A formative rational principle; Plotinus makes relations participate in such principles and in Ideal-Forms."}]},{"i":69,"j":106,"loc":"First Tractate 10–12","title":"Searching for the common ground of Quality","summary":"Plotinus looks for what habit, disposition, passive quality and figure share. Power fails as the common ground, since ugliness, disease and incapacity are not powers and figure is not a power; Reason-Principle fails too, since chronic disease is none. He suggests that Quality is a non-essential characteristic or form imposed on a substance after it exists, with deformity and disease as principles imperfectly present or disturbed. He then criticizes the fourfold division of Quality, proposes dividing qualities by body and soul and by the senses, and asks whether 'boxer' and 'agent' belong to Quality or Relation. He ends by asking whether quality, habit or wisdom can be common to the Intellectual and Sensible realms.","ideas":["Neither power nor Reason-Principle explains every quality","Differences that complete a substance are only analogically qualities","Quality is what comes after the substance and characterizes it","Arts are qualities as they affect the soul, relatives as they look outward"],"terms":[{"t":"quale","d":"The qualified thing, as distinct from the Quality that makes it such."},{"t":"privation","d":"The absence of a Form, used to account for ignorance, disease and incapacity."}]},{"i":107,"j":138,"loc":"First Tractate 13–18","title":"Date, Place, Action and timeless Motion","summary":"Plotinus argues that Date ('yesterday', 'last year') reduces to time and its parts, or else combines several categories, so it needs no category of its own. Place-terms such as 'at Athens' likewise belong to Place, and treating 'being in' as a category would multiply categories without end. Taking up Action, he proposes that the true category is Act, with Motion either a distinct genus or a species of Act marked 'imperfect'. Motion, like Act, is timeless in itself, with time attaching only to its extent; walking is walking from the outset. Neither Act nor Motion reduces to Relation, since each has its character before any relationship, and he raises questions about thinking, walking and passivity.","ideas":["Date and Place collapse into time, place and relation","The category should be Act rather than Action","Motion is imperfect Act aiming at a goal beyond itself, and timeless in itself","Things hold their character prior to any relation they enter"],"terms":[{"t":"Act (energeia)","d":"Actual operation, which Plotinus holds is timeless and prior to Motion."},{"t":"imperfect Act","d":"A description of Motion as an Act that aims at a result distinct from itself."}]},{"i":139,"j":182,"loc":"First Tractate 19–24","title":"Action and Passion, Possession and Situation","summary":"Plotinus asks whether life and happiness, which need duration, are motions, and how Action differs from Passion. Cutting and being cut may be one motion, and writing on a tablet or treading the earth involves no Passion; Passion seems to lie in a supervening effect such as pain, or in alteration for the worse in a thing that contributes nothing. One same motion is Action from the agent's side and Passion from the patient's, which brings the pair close to Relation. Thought and providence are not strictly actions, and footprints are accidental results. He then dismisses Possession and Situation as categories: possession covers everything owned, weapons are substances, and sitting or reclining reduces to place, Act, Passion or Relation.","ideas":["Action may occur without any corresponding Passion","Passion is change toward the worse or not toward the better without the patient's help","One motion counts as Action and Passion from two points of view","Possession and Situation are too narrow and composite to be genera"],"terms":[{"t":"Passion (paschein)","d":"Being acted upon; Plotinus locates it in alteration, often marked by pain, rather than in mere reception of an act."},{"t":"Situation","d":"Position such as sitting or reclining, which Plotinus resolves into place and position."}]},{"i":183,"j":222,"loc":"First Tractate 25–30","title":"Against the fourfold scheme ranking Matter first","summary":"Plotinus attacks a school (the Stoics, though unnamed) that divides things into Substrates, Qualities, States and Relative States under one common 'Something'. He argues that this Something is indefinable, that placing Matter, the potential, first makes actuality impossible, and that their God becomes a composite or a mere state of Matter. Unity and Non-Magnitude must precede Mass, and the trust in sense-perception that leads them to equate the permanent with the Real is criticized. Their Qualities, if bodiless, contradict their materialism and, if material, collapse into Matter, so that only Matter remains, and Matter cannot speak or think. States and Relative States are heterogeneous groups and so cannot make a genus.","ideas":["Putting potential Matter above the actual reverses the order of reality","The One and the unextended must precede extension and plurality","Permanence alone does not define Reality, as a lasting shadow shows","A doctrine that Matter is all leaves no Intellect to know it"],"terms":[{"t":"Substrate","d":"The underlying subject; this school makes Matter the first substrate and principle."},{"t":"Seminal Principles (Logoi)","d":"Formative principles that, on this school's view, would themselves become material."}]},{"i":223,"j":248,"loc":"Second Tractate 1–3","title":"Being as plurality deriving from the One","summary":"Having examined both schemes, Plotinus sets out to give his own view and show its agreement with Plato. True Being is eternal, set apart from Becoming, and is not a single genus divided into Being and Becoming. Being is a plurality in unity, and he asks how genera relate to first-principles, arguing that a single genus would destroy the species. The plurality derives from a unity that is external to the genera: the One is beyond Existence and is not counted among them. He suggests that the genera may be parts of one whole that our thinking divides and then reunites.","ideas":["Being and Becoming do not share a genus, as Socrates and his portrait do not","Being is a diversified unity, many as well as one","The One is the transcendent source and not itself a genus","The genera may be aspects of a single whole that thought divides"],"terms":[{"t":"Becoming","d":"The realm of what comes to be and is never truly Being, namely the world of Sense."},{"t":"the One","d":"The source beyond Existence from which the plurality of Being derives."}]},{"i":249,"j":282,"loc":"Second Tractate 4–8","title":"From Soul to the five primary genera","summary":"As a stone is analysed into substrate, quantity and quality, Plotinus examines Soul to find the genera of the Intellectual realm. Soul is a pluralized unity whose being is life, and which becomes many by striving to contemplate itself. Life, shared with Intellect, yields Motion as a genus alongside Being, then Stability, each separable only in thought. In a vision of Intellect as the hearth of Reality, he shows Being as the goal of intellection, Motion as its activity and Stability as its rest. Distinguishing the three reveals Difference, and their reunion reveals Identity, giving five primary genera.","ideas":["Soul is one and many, a single nature comprising plurality","Being and Motion are one in True Being and separable only in thought","Stability is the goal and abiding state of intellection","Being, Motion, Stability, Identity and Difference are the five primary genera"],"terms":[{"t":"Motion (kinēsis)","d":"The life and Act of Intellect, made a genus alongside Being."},{"t":"Stability (stasis)","d":"The unchanging condition of Being, distinct from both Being and Motion."},{"t":"Identity and Difference","d":"The genera revealed when the others are seen as one and as distinct."}]},{"i":283,"j":315,"loc":"Second Tractate 9–12","title":"Why unity is not a genus","summary":"Plotinus asks whether unity, quantity, quality or relation should join the five genera, and argues at length that unity cannot. A unity with nothing else present can be predicated of nothing, and unity has no differentiae, so it cannot form species. If it follows Being it is last, if simultaneous it is with everything, and if prior it is a Principle rather than a genus. Unity varies by degree among things such as an army, a house and Soul, and tracks perfection rather than Being. All things strive toward unity as their origin and goal, so unity is identified with Goodness. The genera are wholes present in each of their instances, yet exist on their own.","ideas":["Unity lacks differentiae and so produces no species","A Principle of something cannot be its genus","Degree of unity tracks perfection, not degree of Being","Things arise from unity and strive back toward it as the Good"],"terms":[{"t":"differentia","d":"The difference that divides a genus into species, which unity cannot supply."},{"t":"Unity-Absolute","d":"The primary unity, distinct from the derived unity found in Being and in particulars."}]},{"i":316,"j":348,"loc":"Second Tractate 13–18","title":"Excluding Quantity, Quality, goodness and other candidates","summary":"Plotinus explains why other candidates are not primary genera. Number and magnitude are posterior, and he suggests that number may arise from Stability and magnitude from Motion. Quality is a consequent of Substance and not its complement, and the four genera complete Being without qualifying it, since Motion, Stability, Identity and Difference are no later additions. Relation, Place, time, Action, Possession and Situation are composite or remote from Being. Goodness either names the First, which is prior to Substance, or appears in degrees, and beauty, knowledge, intelligence and the virtues fall under Motion or Stability or are posterior species.","ideas":["Primary genera are permanently bound up with Being; number and magnitude come later","The four genera are Being's constituents, not its qualities","Goodness as The First lies beyond Being and is predicated of nothing","Intelligence is Being with all its concomitants, not one genus among others"],"terms":[{"t":"posterior","d":"Coming later in the order of being and therefore unable to be a primary genus."},{"t":"sophrosyne (self-control)","d":"A virtue that Plotinus classes as an Act of Intelligence and a species, not a primary genus."}]},{"i":349,"j":374,"loc":"Second Tractate 19–22","title":"How Intellect unfolds into species and souls","summary":"Plotinus asks how the genera produce species, given that differentiae must come from outside a genus, and warns against sinking genera into their species. He compares universal and particular intellects to a science and its theorems: each is actually itself and potentially the whole. From number in Intellect come Magnitude, Quality, shape, equality and inequality, odd and even, as Identity and Difference work within Quantity and Quality. Intellect is a complete living being holding every Form, and he cites Plato on Intellect seeing the Forms in the complete Living Being and on dividing to infinity. Intellect turned on itself yields the many intellects, turned outward it yields Soul, whose lowest part, like a mirror image, gives Form to Matter.","ideas":["A genus exists both in itself and in its species","Universal Intellect is potentially all particular intellects, as science is all its theorems","Identity and Difference generate figure, equality and number within Intellect","Soul's lower part is an image depending on its original"],"terms":[{"t":"Intellect (nous)","d":"The second hypostasis, identified with Being and containing all Forms as a complete living being."},{"t":"Reason-Principle","d":"The rational form by which Intellect, without discursive reasoning, acts as the best reasoning would."}]},{"i":375,"j":411,"loc":"Third Tractate 1–4","title":"Seeking genera for the Sensible world","summary":"Plotinus turns to Becoming and asks whether the Intellectual genera fit the Sensible realm, concluding that new, merely analogous genera are needed. He excludes Soul from the inquiry, as aliens are left out of a census. He rejects mapping Matter, Form, rest and similarity onto Being, Motion, Stability, Identity and Difference, since Form enters Matter from outside. He proposes Matter, Form, Mixture and attributes, gathering the first three into a pseudo-Substance, followed by Relation, Quantity, Quality and Motion. He then asks what Matter, Form and Composite share, and settles on what is not predicated of another: the source from which other things proceed.","ideas":["Sensible genera are analogues and images of the Intellectual ones","Soul belongs to the Intellectual and is excluded from Sensible Being","Sensible genera: Substance, Relation, Quantity, Quality, Motion","Substance is what is never predicated of anything else"],"terms":[{"t":"pseudo-substance","d":"The so-called Substance of the Sensible realm, which is properly Becoming."},{"t":"Couplement","d":"The composite of Matter and Form."}]},{"i":412,"j":441,"loc":"Third Tractate 5–8","title":"The derived Being of sensible substance","summary":"Plotinus refines the definition of Substance as not present in a subject, noting that Form is not in Matter as in a subject, nor Man in Socrates. Predicating Man of Socrates is like saying whiteness is white. He argues that fire and earth are substances by 'existing', but sensible existence is derived, as white depends on whiteness and ultimately on the Supreme Being. Matter cannot give existence to what is in it, since the higher gives being to the lower, and Being differs in Matter, Form and Composite, so Substance is not their genus. Sensible Substance is best taken as the whole amalgam of qualities and Matter, an imitation of True Substance traced on a shadow.","ideas":["Parts of a composite are not 'in' it as in a subject","Sensible being exists by participation in Authentic Being","Matter is a shadow of a Principle and cannot ground existence","Sensible Substance is a sketch on a sterile ground"],"terms":[{"t":"present in a subject","d":"The relation of an attribute to its bearer, which Plotinus denies of Form in Matter."},{"t":"Authentic Being","d":"True Being, on which the being of sensibles depends."}]},{"i":442,"j":474,"loc":"Third Tractate 9–12","title":"Species of body; great and small","summary":"Plotinus divides Sensible Substance, identified with body, into material bodies (the four elements) and organic bodies of plants and animals, subdivided by habitation, weight and position in the universe. He denies that the universal is deficient compared with the particular: Socrates receives manhood from Man, and the individual is prior only in our knowing. Division may also proceed by paired qualities such as hot-dry, and by shapes, since sensible substance is an assemblage of sensibles. Turning to Quantity, he assigns Time and Space to Relation and argues that great and small, many and few, are real quantities with a Reason-Principle of greatness, just as beautiful is absolute while more beautiful is relative. Hence Quantity admits contrariety.","ideas":["Bodies divide into elemental and organic, then by place and form","The universal is prior by nature; the individual only for our knowledge","Greatness is absolute by participation in the Great; 'greater' is relative","Quantity has contraries: great and small, many and few"],"terms":[{"t":"Form-Idea","d":"The ideal type, such as Man, by which sensible bodies are best classified."},{"t":"contrariety","d":"Opposition of contraries, which Plotinus allows to Quantity."}]},{"i":475,"j":512,"loc":"Third Tractate 13–16","title":"Figures, similarity, and Quality in the Sensible","summary":"Plotinus distinguishes continuous from discrete quantity and treats line, surface and solid as species of magnitude. He asks whether the straight line, triangle and sphere belong to Quantity or Quality, concluding that figures are differentiated magnitudes, since geometry studies magnitudes, though an object may fall under both categories. Equality is characteristic of Quantity, and similarity may apply to it in another sense. Sensible Substance is more a quale than an essence: the visible Socrates is like a portrait of the Reason-Principle. Sensible Quality is only equivocally related to Intellectual Quality; earthly arts and social virtues belong to it, while Soul itself is restored to the higher realm.","ideas":["Figures are varieties of magnitude qualified by disposition","Geometry is the study of magnitudes, not of Quality","The visible man is a likeness of his Reason-Principle","Qualities are bodiless affections of Soul shown in body"],"terms":[{"t":"continuous and discrete","d":"Kinds of quantity distinguished by having a common or a separate limit."},{"t":"apostasy","d":"Soul's turning away toward body, with which Sensible qualities are associated."}]},{"i":513,"j":545,"loc":"Third Tractate 17–20","title":"Dividing Quality: differentiae and contraries","summary":"Plotinus asks how Quality can be divided into species when its differentiae cannot be qualities. Division by sense-organs makes no difference in the objects, and division by effects or powers suits only the sciences. He concludes that looking for differences of differences is futile; sense and intelligence show diversity without explaining it. Bitter and sweet may differ by Relation, and five exceeds three rather than differing from it. He classifies negations, privations and passive states, holding that a lasting state like being red is a quality but becoming red is a motion. On contraries, he rejects 'greatest difference' as a definition and proposes that contraries are qualities with no specific likeness.","ideas":["Quality is never a differentia of Quality","Perception and intelligence indicate difference but do not explain it","States are qualities; processes of becoming are motions","Contraries are things lacking any formal similarity, not merely farthest apart"],"terms":[{"t":"privation","d":"An absence such as blindness, which is itself counted a quality."},{"t":"contrary","d":"An opposite quality; Plotinus rejects defining it as the greatest difference."}]},{"i":546,"j":572,"loc":"Third Tractate 21–23","title":"Motion as a genus of the Sensible","summary":"Plotinus sets the conditions for Motion to be a genus and argues it cannot be reduced to Substance, Quality, Action, Passion or Relation, since it has a reality prior to its attribution. Change is not a higher genus; becoming is a distinct species of motion. Motion is roughly the passage from potentiality to actuality, whether producing a new Form or being the pure Form of a potentiality, as walking or dancing. Its constant element is perpetual difference, never resting in identity, and Time is the measure that accompanies it. Sensible Motion enters from outside, an image of Life, passing from agent to patient like a breath and taking its quality from the moved.","ideas":["Motion has its own reality and is not a mere relative","Motion is the progression from potentiality to actuality","Difference is predicated of all Motion; Time measures it","Motion is qualified by its origin, its course and the thing moved"],"terms":[{"t":"potentiality","d":"The capacity, such as the ability to walk, whose realization is Motion."},{"t":"alteration","d":"Qualitative change, treated as a species of Motion."}]},{"i":573,"j":606,"loc":"Third Tractate 24–28","title":"Kinds of motion, Rest, and conclusion","summary":"Plotinus examines locomotion, finding its unity in the tendency to seek an appointed place, with differences of direction and shape coming from outside. Integration and disintegration begin as locomotion but are distinct experiences, and reducing all alteration to them would abolish qualities such as colour, and cannot explain learning. Motions may be divided as natural, artificial and purposive. Stability belongs to the Intellectual, while sensible Rest is only the negation of a particular motion and has no generic status. He closes by recalling that Action and Passion are motions, that the other so-called genera reduce to his own, and that his investigation into the kinds of Being is complete.","ideas":["Locomotion is one species, distinguished by seeking a proper place","Integration and disintegration cannot replace alteration","Sensible Rest is a negation of motion, unlike Intellectual Stability","The remaining categories reduce to the genera already posited"],"terms":[{"t":"locomotion","d":"Change of place, whose species differ by direction and course."},{"t":"Rest","d":"In the Sensible realm, the mere absence of a due motion rather than a genus."}]},{"i":607,"j":627,"loc":"Fourth Tractate 1–3","title":"How the soul and Being are everywhere","summary":"The Fourth Tractate opens by asking how soul, which has no magnitude, can be present throughout the material universe. Plotinus rejects the model of a quality like colour spread over a mass, since soul is numerically one in foot and hand. He argues that the Authentic All is contained in nothing and needs no place, so the visible universe does not reach it from a distance but meets it at every point. Against the view that Being stays apart and sends out only powers or rays, he holds that a placeless principle must be present entire wherever it is present at all, each recipient taking only what it can.","ideas":["Soul's presence is omnipresence, not distribution part by part","The Authentic All needs no place; the universe circles about it and finds it everywhere","What is placeless is present whole or not at all; fragmenting it would make it body","Recipients take only as much as their capacity allows"],"terms":[{"t":"the Authentic All","d":"Real Being taken as a whole, prior to and containing the visible universe."},{"t":"omnipresence","d":"Being present entire at every point without being divided among them."}]},{"i":628,"j":669,"loc":"Fourth Tractate 4–10","title":"Many souls in one; the hand and light","summary":"Plotinus faces the objection that there are many souls and many intelligences, which seems to clash with one omnipresent identity. He answers that diversity in the Intellectual order comes from differentiation, not spatial separation; the many souls existed before body, distinct without partition, like items of knowledge in one mind. Images of a hand controlling a long plank and a light at the centre of a transparent sphere show a power reaching over a whole area without being spread over it. He then argues that powers cut off from their source would fade, as a reflection does, so the source must be present with its powers; analogies of portraits and warmed objects do not show otherwise.","ideas":["Soul was one and many before bodies existed","A partless power can govern a whole extent, like a hand or a central light","An image in water or mirror depends on its original and cannot survive apart from it","The emanants of the First, souls and Intellect, are imperishable"],"terms":[{"t":"Intellectual-Principle","d":"Intellect, identified with Being and the Beings, one yet many by differentiation."},{"t":"emanant power","d":"A power going out from a higher principle, which remains attached to its source."}]},{"i":670,"j":707,"loc":"Fourth Tractate 11–16","title":"Participation by fitness; the two men in us","summary":"If Being is present everywhere, the differing ranks of participants come from their fitness, as transparent and opaque things answer differently to light. A spoken word heard whole by every ear in range illustrates how soul is entire at each point; bodies come to soul rather than soul entering them. Plotinus asks what we are: before birth we were pure souls in the Intellectual, but another man seeking birth has wound himself about us, so we have become dual. The body's unrest is likened to an unruly crowd drowning out the Elders, and he reinterprets the ancients' talk of descents, punishments and Hades as degrees of communion with body.","ideas":["Presence depends on the recipient's adequacy, not on place","A word sounding through the air is entire for every listener","The original Man in us is overlaid by a later man of birth","Descent means the soul giving something of itself to body, not moving into it"],"terms":[{"t":"image-soul","d":"The lower soul bound to body, which can go to the lower place while the true soul stays free."},{"t":"kosmos of life","d":"The self-gathered whole of life in which every newcomer participates entire."}]},{"i":708,"j":730,"loc":"Fifth Tractate 1–6","title":"One god in all; the radii and centre","summary":"The Fifth Tractate continues the theme, starting from the common conviction that the god in each of us is one and the same in all. Plotinus traces the quest for the good to a striving for unity, and warns that discursive reason, borrowing its principles from body, will wrongly picture Real Being as a thing of parts. Reasoning from the essence of Being itself, unchanging and placeless, he concludes that it is present to all while lodged in nothing. The Intellectual Beings are compared to centres coinciding in one centre, the radii leading to them rather than producing them, and the Idea of Man to a single seal making many impressions.","ideas":["All things seek the good because all seek unity","Intellectual matters must be reasoned from intellectual principles, not bodily ones","The many Beings are like centres that are one in the first centre","The multiple is absorbed in the Absolute, not the Absolute in the multiple"],"terms":[{"t":"Authentic Existent","d":"Real Being, eternal, undivided and set in no place."}]},{"i":731,"j":764,"loc":"Fifth Tractate 7–12","title":"Ideas, Matter, love, and becoming the All","summary":"Plotinus says we are those Real Beings and so all things are one; looking inward, as if turned by Athene, we would see God, ourselves and the All. Matter participates in the Ideas without the Ideas leaving their place: one Idea of Fire shapes all sensible fire. The soul's unity covers the cosmos however large, which explains why some called it a self-increasing number. The One abides in itself like the Love waiting at the door, and wisdom and the good are shared whole by all. Eternity is to time as a centre to its radius, and anyone who stops seeking a limited spot and puts away what is alien becomes the All.","ideas":["Matter touches the Idea at every point though the Idea stays apart","One Idea, not many, forms every fire","Lovers do not possess beauty but wait before it","One grows by casting off the alien, not by adding to oneself"],"terms":[{"t":"Love Waiting at the Door","d":"Image for the many longing for the self-enclosed Beauty that they attain as a whole."},{"t":"aiōn (eternity)","d":"Unchanging identity, compared to the centre from which time runs out as a line."}]},{"i":765,"j":800,"loc":"Sixth Tractate 1–5","title":"Multiplicity, the unlimited, and what Number is","summary":"The Sixth Tractate, On Numbers, begins with the suggestion that multiplicity is a falling away from unity and extension a kind of exile. The universe is beautiful because unity holds it, not because of its size. Plotinus asks how number can be infinite, describes the unlimited as something that slips away from every definition, and turns to whether Numbers in the Intellectual are Ideas or mere concomitants of the Ideas. He sets Plato's two remarks side by side, number learned from day and night versus number as a real Being, and argues that unity and the decad must exist before the things in which they are observed.","ideas":["A thing is most itself when it holds to unity, not when it spreads","Extension serves as Matter for Beauty","The unlimited appears as either of two opposites and is fixed by neither","Unity predicated of things must exist prior to them"],"terms":[{"t":"apeiron (the unlimited)","d":"What has no limit of itself and receives limit only from outside."},{"t":"henad","d":"A unit or unity, here considered as a real Intellectual being."}]},{"i":801,"j":838,"loc":"Sixth Tractate 6–11","title":"Number prior to the Beings","summary":"Plotinus first explains how Ideas exist: Justice or Movement is not produced by being thought, since in the Intellectual the thing is itself a thought. All the Intellectual Beings are held in one nature, ordered as Being, then Intellect, then the Living-Form. He argues that Number cannot depend on the Living-Form or on Intellect, and concludes that Number pre-exists within Being and caused its division: Being is Number gathered, the Beings Number unfolded. The Forms are therefore called Henads and Numbers, and counting in the mind is an image of this true Number. A man wishing for so much gold shows that the quantity comes before what is quantified.","ideas":["Justice There is an activity of Intellect, lovelier than evening or dawn","Number existed in Being potentially and divided it","The Forms are Henads and Numbers; arithmetical number is their image","The number of the Beings is a cause, not a chance"],"terms":[{"t":"Living-Form (Absolute Living Being)","d":"The complete Intellectual animal that contains all living things."},{"t":"monadic number","d":"Number as a set of units, the image of the Authentic Number."}]},{"i":839,"j":864,"loc":"Sixth Tractate 12–15","title":"Answering those who make unity a mental attitude","summary":"An objector argues that unity, like crowd, festival or above, is only a notion produced in us by objects. Plotinus replies that unity cannot be derived from physical presentations, that the mind affirming unity is itself a unity, and that unity's degrees, greater in the continuous, point to a real principle of Unity. Duality is not a mere relation, since it arises both from joining and from splitting; things are one by Unity as white by Whiteness. He then places Number in the Collective Being: Being lays down the numbers of its offspring before producing them, so Number is source of actuality to the Beings.","ideas":["Nothing can be named or conceived without one or some number","Unity has degrees, so it is a real nature","Duality comes from Duality, not from association or division","Being produces the Beings according to Number"],"terms":[{"t":"Unity (to hen)","d":"The real principle by whose presence each thing is one."}]},{"i":865,"j":893,"loc":"Sixth Tractate 16–18","title":"Essential versus counted number; infinite number","summary":"An objector asks where these numbers fit among the genera and how they relate to quantity. Plotinus distinguishes the number we produce by counting separate things, which is mere quantity, from essential number inherent in a thing's being, as in a choir or in virtue as a Tetrad; our counting draws on the number of our own essence. Number is limited; infinity lies only in our power to conceive more. He allows an infinite Line in the Intellectual, without quantity, and places the primal figures There, such as the Pyramid of Fire. The tractate closes with praise of the Absolute Living-Form's untainted life and of Being, which Parmenides rightly made one with Unity.","ideas":["Counting produces quantity as walking produces movement","Soul's essence is said to be number and harmony","Real number has no boundary because it needs no measure; every Being is Measure","Being exists forever and so its products do too"],"terms":[{"t":"essential number","d":"Number belonging to a thing's being, not produced by our reckoning."},{"t":"the Intellectual Pyramid","d":"The primal figure of Fire There, which sensible fire tries to reproduce."}]},{"i":894,"j":918,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 1–3","title":"No planning in the divine making","summary":"The Seventh Tractate asks why souls sent to birth were given senses, and whether this was a reasoned provision against danger. Plotinus argues that there is no planning There: reasoning stands in for a missing higher power, while the divine Act is complete and contains the future as present. In the Intellectual the thing is identical with its cause, so each Idea carries the reason for its being, as Man There is complete from eternity. Eyes and eyebrows exist for completeness, and the cause is part of the essence. This raises the difficulty of how sense-perception could belong to Man There.","ideas":["Providence means the world is as wise planning would have made it","In the Intellectual, essence and cause are one","The why of each Idea is inherent in it","Planning about the eternal is impossible"],"terms":[{"t":"pronoia (providence)","d":"Here, not foresight in time but the completeness of the divine Act."}]},{"i":919,"j":949,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 4–8","title":"Man here and Man There; animals in Intellect","summary":"Plotinus examines what man is, rejecting the soul-body conjoint and the reasoning soul alone, and settles on a Reason-Principle working through soul. There are grades of man: the higher Man from the more godlike soul, the man of birth, and a lower phase, each of us acting by one of these. Sense There is a clearer perception of the Intellectual counterparts, and our perceptions are dim intellections. The soul may produce an animal's form when its condition fails, like a craftsman limited by his material. Animals exist There because the universe after the One must be many, and an Intellect including all lives cannot contain only man.","ideas":["The man of this world is a reduced image of a higher Man","Perceptions here are dim intellections; intellections There are vivid perceptions","The Idea of horse exists before any plan to make horses","What comes after the One must be a manifold"],"terms":[{"t":"logos (Reason-Principle)","d":"A forming principle, here what constitutes man in each grade of soul."},{"t":"daimon (Celestial)","d":"A higher being of one Form with the soul that makes man, in Plato's sense."}]},{"i":950,"j":985,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 9–13","title":"Unreasoning life, earth and fire There","summary":"Objectors ask how the divine can include the unreasoning. Plotinus answers that what is unreasoning here was a Reason-Principle There; lives differ by degrees of clarity, and Intellect does not lose itself by appearing as horse, while horn and talon supply what the lower life lacks. Plants, earth and fire also exist There as living Reason-Principles: stones grow within the earth as twigs on a tree, and fire comes from a fiery life, not from friction. Since the world is a copy, the higher heaven teems with every kind of life. Intellect, holding Identity and Difference, moves through all things in a stationary wandering within the Meadow of Truth.","ideas":["The unintelligent here is Intellect in a particular mode There","Earth There is living, the source of the earth-principle that shapes stones","Water and air hide their soul because they are always flowing","Intellect wanders within itself through varied Being"],"terms":[{"t":"Meadow of Truth","d":"The varied Intellectual realm through which Intellect moves without straying from itself."},{"t":"Identity and Difference","d":"The two Kinds by which Intellect is one and yet holds distinct beings."}]},{"i":986,"j":1023,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 14–18","title":"Intellect filled by the Good","summary":"Intellect is a multiple unity like a Reason-Principle, and its inner Sympathy is unity, not the copy of it found among separated things here. Plotinus praises that all-including Life, a globe of living faces, and turns to its source. Intellect did not see the Good as many; it turned towards it, was filled, and broke the single power it received into variety. The Good, like the sun, causes both the being and the knowing of the Intellectual, being itself neither. Intellect is determined Life, a vestige of a formless First. Plotinus then asks how the Ideas are good, holding that each has goodness by derivation, the Good being present in them in the mode of parts.","ideas":["Intellect could not hold the Good's power whole and divided it","The Good is to Intellect as the sun to visible things and sight","The First must be without Form, since Intellect is Form going into plurality","Every Idea is good because its life comes from the First"],"terms":[{"t":"the Good","d":"The First, source of Being and Intellect, above both."},{"t":"Universal Sympathy","d":"The unity of all things There, of which worldly sympathy is a copy."}]},{"i":1024,"j":1064,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 19–25","title":"What makes things good; the soul's love","summary":"Plotinus rejects desire as the test of the good and tries judgement through opposites such as order and disorder, which point to Intellect, life and soul. Yet Intellect is not the Good, since all things look to the Good and not all to Intellect. Life and Intellect are loved for the light cast upon them from the Good; touched by it, the soul becomes Love and rises, as living faces attract more than symmetrical dead ones. The Good, self-sufficing and needing nothing, has produced and preserves all. A critic's challenge about whether existence is good leads to the Philebus, and Plotinus answers that each higher thing is the good of the one below, up to the First Good.","ideas":["Beauty is what irradiates symmetry, not symmetry itself","The soul is stirred only when the light of the Good falls on Intellect","The good is pursued because it is good, not good because pursued","Matter's good is Form, body's soul, soul's virtue, then Intellect, then the First"],"terms":[{"t":"Philebus","d":"Plato's dialogue placing pleasure in the mixed good, which Plotinus says concerns our good, not the First."}]},{"i":1065,"j":1098,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 26–30","title":"Pleasure, Form, and the good of each","summary":"Anyone who attains the good knows it; pleasure fails as a test because it demands endless novelty and holds nothing. The good of each thing is a Form, not merely what is apt to it but its better self, a portion of the Good. Plotinus asks whether Matter could desire the good, and concludes that the good advances away from Matter, so the highest good is wholly free of it. He answers the critic who feels nothing at talk of the Good: even rejecting it implies some notion of good. Finally he argues that Intellect and pleasure do not blend, and that talk of Intellect joined with pleasure is a metaphor for unhindered activity.","ideas":["Pleasure follows attainment of the good but does not constitute it","What a thing lacks of its better is its good","The higher the ascent, the more Form and the less Matter","The truly pleasant lies in the realm above, source of all brightness"],"terms":[{"t":"eidos (Form)","d":"What approaches each thing as its good, raising it to a higher order."}]},{"i":1099,"j":1121,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 31–36","title":"The formless Beauty and the soul's union","summary":"The soul, loving the Supreme like a lover seeking a beloved, passes over the beauty of the world as made of flesh and Matter. The source of all beauty must be formless, not lacking shape but the source of even Intellectual shape, a beauty above beauty loved with an infinite love. Shape is an impress from the unshaped, and love is born when the lover forms an immaterial image within. Plotinus describes the union: the soul lays aside every shape, sees the presence within with nothing between, and wants nothing else. Intellect too has a power of loving, drunk with nectar; the way to the Good is by analogies, abstractions and the virtues, until the quester becomes the radiance itself.","ideas":["The First is formless as the source of all Form","In union there is no duality but a two in one","Intellect knows its content and loves its transcendent","The final vision is a light that is itself the seeing"],"terms":[{"t":"formless Form","d":"The First, beautiful because free of any shape."},{"t":"the grand learning","d":"Knowledge of or contact with the Good, reached by purification and ascent."}]},{"i":1122,"j":1161,"loc":"Seventh Tractate 37–42","title":"The First does not think","summary":"Plotinus argues against those who give the First intellection, even of itself alone. If its grandeur lay in thinking it would gain worth from thinking, and to be essence and intellective act would make it a duality; Plato, he says, puts it above Intellect. The First cannot even say I am or I am good, since that affirms Being. Intellection needs difference, a knower, an object and movement, so it begins with Intellect and Being; the First keeps an august repose, while providence is saved by its being the source of all. The Good needs no light, as the eye needs none. The tractate ends with the King of All passage: everything is linked to the Supreme through intermediaries.","ideas":["Intellection would introduce duality into the simplex","Intellection is an aid to weaker beings, an eye to the blind","Know yourself suits the manifold, not the First","Secondaries depend on the First, tertiaries on secondaries, the sense-world on soul"],"terms":[{"t":"noēsis (intellection)","d":"The act of thinking, identical with Being, and below the Good."},{"t":"the King of All","d":"The Platonic phrase read as naming the First, about whom all things are."}]},{"i":1162,"j":1186,"loc":"Eighth Tractate, 1–4","title":"What it means for something to be in our power","summary":"The Eighth Tractate, On Free-Will and the Will of the One, asks whether the gods, and above all the One, have freedom, but begins with freedom in ourselves. Plotinus defines the voluntary as what is done without compulsion and with knowledge. He then tests where freedom could lie: not in rage or appetite, which animals and madmen share, nor in fancy driven by bodily states, nor in mere awareness. He traces self-disposal to will, will to right reasoning joined with knowledge, and finally to the activity of the Intellectual-Principle. Against the objection that Intellect acts by its nature, he argues that movement towards a recognised good is no compulsion and that where being and act are one there is no master and mastered.","ideas":["The voluntary is what we do unforced and knowingly; ignorance of a particular undermines it","Appetite, passion and bodily fancy cannot ground freedom, since they arise from an extern","Freedom springs from the Intellectual-Principle, highest in us; the gods living by it are free","Servitude is being barred from one's own good, not being held from harm"],"terms":[{"t":"self-disposal","d":"Being master of one's own act, with no outside power directing it."},{"t":"Intellectual-Principle (nous)","d":"Intellect, the second principle, whose activity is the source of freedom in us."}]},{"i":1187,"j":1207,"loc":"Eighth Tractate, 5–7","title":"Freedom in virtue and in The Good","summary":"Plotinus asks whether soul acting by virtue is free, since brave or just deeds depend on outside occasions such as war or wrong; virtue, like a physician wishing no patients, would prefer inaction. He concludes that freedom lies not in the outward deed but in the inner activity, the Intellection and vision of virtue, which may even sacrifice life, means and country to its aim. True will in the highest Intellect is identical with intellection and rests in the good it seeks. He then turns to the First: it would be rash to call the Good a product of chance or unfree because it acts by its nature. Such a view would empty the very word freedom, and it would make the most good the least free.","ideas":["Virtue's acts are prompted by circumstance, so freedom must lie in its inner vision","Freedom belongs to the unembodied; will is free whatever orders it gives to the outer","Soul is free by moving through Intellect towards The Good, which is the source of all self-disposal","The Good is neither chance-made nor constrained by its nature, since in it act and being are one"],"terms":[{"t":"aretē (virtue)","d":"Treated here as a mode of Intellectual-Principle, a second Intellect lifting the soul."},{"t":"The Good","d":"The First Principle, sole object of desire, from which all else takes its power of self-disposal."}]},{"i":1208,"j":1229,"loc":"Eighth Tractate, 8–11","title":"The First did not happen to be","summary":"Plotinus warns that freedom and self-disposal are not attributes of the First but lesser terms borrowed from lesser things. He rejects the claim that the First merely happened to be what it is: happening and chance belong to the multiple and to process, while the First has no origin. It is determined only by its uniqueness, being what it must be by a necessity prior to all necessities, yet without a Must; it does not even admit a Thus. Chance, the opposite of reason, cannot be the author of reason, order and limit. The First stays as it is not from powerlessness but because so is best, and inquiry into it must end in silence, for it has no cause, quality or place.","ideas":["Freedom is said of the First only by analogy, borrowed from lower things","Happening belongs to what receives existence and then an addition; the Source received nothing","What eliminates chance from everything else cannot itself be chance","To conceive God we must abolish space, placing Him before all environment"],"terms":[{"t":"Happening","d":"Chance or accident in a thing's being, which Plotinus denies of the First."},{"t":"Transcendent of Being","d":"The First as prior to Being, so that even Manner of Being cannot attach to it."}]},{"i":1230,"j":1255,"loc":"Eighth Tractate, 12–16","title":"The One as self-willed and self-caused","summary":"Answering the lingering doubt whether God is subject to what He is, Plotinus contrasts us, compounds whose reality is partly other than ourselves, with the First, which is sovereign over itself and is the liberator of all else. Conceding the use of terms not strictly apt, he argues that if there is will in the Supreme, will and essence are identical, so He is as He willed to be. Every being seeks to become its good; the Good itself must be a willing towards itself and could prefer nothing else. Further arguments follow: the simplex is untouched by accident, the First is cause of reason and of Himself, love and self-love in one, and everywhere and nowhere. He is an eternal waking without an awakener, existing as He acted Himself into being.","ideas":["In the One will and essence are one, so He is what He wills to be","Things carrying their reason within arise by plan, not hazard; their source is cause of itself","The Supreme is love and self-love, seeker and sought at once","Becoming wholly that inner state, we too are more than free"],"terms":[{"t":"self-mastery","d":"Strictly implies a duality of act and essence, so only loosely applied to the pure unity."},{"t":"\"so to speak\"","d":"The qualifier Plotinus asks the reader to supply to every term applied to the Supreme."}]},{"i":1256,"j":1280,"loc":"Eighth Tractate, 17–21","title":"Cause of the cause, wholly self-willing","summary":"Plotinus argues that the ordered universe implies an Intellectual-Principle containing all Reason-Principles, a concordant multiple that cannot be chance; its Source, a partless One above it, can derive neither being nor quality from elsewhere. Using the image of a centre whose radii and circle unfold it, he presents Intellect as an image circling about its motionless prior, and the One as cause of the cause. He cites Plato on the necessary as far removed from hazard. To the objection that a self-maker must exist before itself, he replies that God is pure maker, His Activity is His reality, and His self-making is eternal and simultaneous with Him. He could not have made Himself otherwise, since He is will throughout; the reader is told to add nothing to Him.","ideas":["The Intellectual Kosmos of all Reason-Principles is ordered, so its Source is far from chance","Centre, radii and circle picture the One, Intellect and Being","The first Activity is the very reality; God's self-making has no before","God is free, subject not even to His own law"],"terms":[{"t":"Reason-Principle (logos)","d":"The formative plan of a thing, contained in Intellect before the universe."},{"t":"Activity (energeia)","d":"Act, which in the First is identical with its reality, not added to an essence."}]},{"i":1281,"j":1319,"loc":"Ninth Tractate, 1–4","title":"Unity is not Soul, Being or Intellect","summary":"The Ninth Tractate, On the Good, or the One, opens with the claim that beings are beings by unity: army, chorus, house, plant, health, beauty and virtue all depend on it. Soul gives unity but receives it too, being a manifold of powers. Nor can unity be Being or the Intellectual-Principle, since Being contains life, intelligence and the Forms, and Intellect is dual as thinker and thought and looks to a prior. The Unity is formless, not in motion or at rest, and even calling it Cause names our experience, not its nature. Since knowing is multiple, awareness of it comes by a presence beyond knowledge; teaching can only point the road.","ideas":["Everything exists in the degree that it has unity","Soul, Being and Intellect are manifolds and so only participate in unity","The mind fears the formless and slips back to sense","The One is reached by a presence above knowing, through the summit of Intellect"],"terms":[{"t":"The Unity (to hen)","d":"The One, the First, prior to Being and to all form."},{"t":"Intellectual Kosmos","d":"The ordered whole of the Forms in Intellect, a unity like that of a world."}]},{"i":1320,"j":1345,"loc":"Ninth Tractate, 5–8","title":"The ascent inward to the centre","summary":"Addressing those who accept something beyond body, Plotinus leads from soul to Intellect, a concentrated plurality near the First but not simplex, and from it to the Unity. The name Unity is only roughly apt: it is not the oneness of point or monad but infinite in power, self-sufficing, without need, will or Intellection, a Beyond-Good. To see it the soul must be emptied of all impress, as Matter is of quality, and turn wholly inward; Minos is cited as the Familiar of Zeus who made laws from such converse. The soul moves like a circle about its own centre, and all centres coincide in the centre of all. The Supreme is always present; we are like a choir that must face its conductor to sing well.","ideas":["The Unity's partlessness is greatness of power, not smallness","The One needs nothing, is not a good to itself, and has no Intellection","The soul must become formless and turn inward to receive the One","We are always before the Supreme but do not always look, like a turned-away choir"],"terms":[{"t":"Beyond-Good","d":"The One as above even good, good only to those that share in it."},{"t":"otherness","d":"Difference, which alone separates unbodied beings; put away, they are present to each other."}]},{"i":1346,"j":1363,"loc":"Ninth Tractate, 9–11","title":"Union with the One: the flight of solitary to solitary","summary":"Facing the Supreme, the soul sees the wellspring of Life, Intellect, Being and Good, which gives forever without being lessened. Plotinus describes the soul's inborn love by the myth of Aphrodite and Eros: heavenly There, vulgar here, like a daughter who leaves a noble father and later returns. In the vision the soul becomes light and one with the Supreme, centre coinciding with centre, so that seer and seen are not two; it falls back only through the body and reasoning. The rule of the Mysteries, nothing divulged to the uninitiate, reflects this: the one who has seen leaves the temple images behind for the inner sanctuary. The Ennead closes with the life of the gods as the passing of solitary to solitary.","ideas":["The emanants are eternal because the Source gives without diminution","The soul's love is an Aphrodite, heavenly or vulgar, seeking its father","In union there is no duality, which is why the vision cannot be told","Fallen back, we rise again by virtue to Intellect and to the Supreme"],"terms":[{"t":"henōsis (union)","d":"The soul's becoming one with the Supreme, beyond seeing and knowing."},{"t":"Aphrodite of the heavens","d":"The soul's higher love, contrasted with the earthly Aphrodite of the public ways."}]}]});